  "analysis": "We have to compute, for a given permutation p of size n with no fixed points, the minimum and maximum possible number of balls not receiving a gift when exactly k balls forget theirs. Each ball i gets a present iff both i and its preimage under p (the unique x with p[x] = i) did not forget.\n\nThis decomposes by cycles of the permutation. For a cycle of length L, if z balls in that cycle forget (0 <= z <= L), then the number of balls getting a present in that cycle is at most:\n- if z = 0: L get\n- if 1 <= z <= L-1: L - z - 1 get\n- if z = L: 0 get\nThus the number not getting in that cycle is L - (max possible gets). Across all cycles, total not-getting is the sum of per-cycle values.\n\nWe need to distribute the total k forgetful balls among cycles to either minimize or maximize the total not-getting.\n- For the maximum not-getting (i.e., minimize total got), we view the problem as distributing k increments (adding zeros) with per-cycle marginal costs: first zero in a cycle costs 2 (increase not-get by 2), subsequent zeros costs 1 each, and the very last zero to completely fill a cycle costs 0 (no further not-get change). The optimal strategy to maximize not-getting is to spread zeros to create as many first zeros as possible, then fill second zeros, but never exceed the per-cycle capacity. A compact formula emerges:\n  - Let m be the number of cycles, and sum_cap1 = sum over cycles of max(0, L_i - 2).\n  - First, allocate first zeros to as many cycles as possible: first_incs = min(k, m). They contribute 2 each to not-getting, so base = 2 * first_incs.\n  - Remaining zeros r = k - first_incs can contribute up to sum_cap1 (each adds 1 to not-getting). So extra = min(r, sum_cap1).\n  - max_not_get = base + extra.\n- For the minimum not-getting (i.e., maximize total gets), it is optimal to pack zeros into as few cycles as possible and, when needed, fill cycles completely before moving to the next. The strategy: sort cycle lengths in increasing order and greedily fill cycles fully (z = L) as long as possible; for the final partially filled cycle (if any), add z zeros with cost h = z + 1. The sum of these costs across cycles equals the minimum not-getting.\n  - Sort L_i ascending. Let remaining k_rem = k. For each L in ascending order:\n    - If k_rem >= L, allocate z = L and add h += L; k_rem -= L.\n    - Else (0 < k_rem < L), add h += k_rem + 1; k_rem = 0; stop.\n  - The final min_not_get is the accumulated h.\n\nOverall, O(n) to find cycles and O(m log m) to sort cycle lengths (m <= n). This handles n up to 1e6 comfortably in C++.",
